//! lotus/stereo_fold.rye -- ALES134: the wavefolder carried into stereo, ONE SHARED TRIANGLE, the reflecting corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class. ALES80 named `wavefold` -- a pre-gain into a ceiling where
//! the excess that would cross the ceiling REFLECTS back down rather than pinning: a triangle in the boosted magnitude with period 2-ceil, the West-Coast fold that turns a plain tone into a bright metallic
//! swarm, for a mono Clip. Where ALES132's hard clip builds a wall, the fold builds a mirror. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and
//! RIGHT hold the SAME length. This rung carries the fold into stereo the plain way, ALES132/ALES133's sibling: run ALES80's proven mono `wavefold` on each channel with the SAME gain and ceiling, so one
//! fold lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The nonlinear class holds exactly as ALES132 stated it, honestly: a fold is still NOT linear, so a shared triangle does NOT preserve the inter-channel ratio in general -- a 1:2 master folded through a
//! ceiling can land BOTH channels on the same value (a 1000/2000 field folded through 1500 gives 1000/1000), the ratio collapsing not by pinning but by REFLECTION. That is not a bug to design around; it is
//! what a stereo fold IS. What the shared map DOES keep, per channel, is the ceiling and sign discipline (a fold is odd, holding the sign and staying within ±ceil); and an identical-channel master still
//! folds to an identical-channel output. Where the hard drive pins the excess, the fold reflects it -- the two agree below the ceiling and diverge above.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadGain (a zero denominator, a below-unity gain, an oversized numerator), BadCeiling (a ceiling outside [1, sample_max]), and BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE
//! either channel is mutated -- every fault depends only on the shared parameters or the shared length, so each check is made ONCE up front, and a refusal never folds one channel and leaves the other dry;
//! otherwise wavefold both channels over [start, start+count) by num/den into ceil via ALES80's `wavefold`. FoldError reused whole.
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE STEREO FOLD LAW: each channel equals ALES80's mono `wavefold` with the same (num, den, ceil) over the same span byte for byte -- a boosted sample below the ceiling passing clean,
//! the excess above reflecting back down the triangle, per channel. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length (a fold writes values only). THE
//! PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW: a fold is nonlinear, so a shared triangle does NOT hold the inter-channel ratio (a 1:2 master folded through a ceiling landing both channels on one value, the ratio collapsing by
//! reflection) -- unlike the linear amplitude class; yet the ceiling and sign discipline hold per channel, an identical-channel master stays identical, and where the hard drive pins the excess the fold
//! reflects it. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal (BadGain, BadCeiling, BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; a unity fold whose samples all sit below the
//! ceiling is the identity on both, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES134.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-033358_fill-ales134-lotus-stereo-fold.md
//! Stands on: lotus/fold.rye (ALES80) -- the mono wavefolder run once per channel, itself ALES78's pre-gain reflected off ALES49's ceiling; lotus/stereo_drive.rye (ALES132) -- the hard-drive sibling this
//! mirrors; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES80's own wavefold, never a length,
//! never a byte fabricated; the drive gain is a rational num/den (unity = 1/1), the ceiling a magnitude in sample units NOT decibels, the fold a piecewise-linear triangle, instantaneous (no attack/release,
//! no anti-aliasing). No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_fold.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const drive = @import("drive.rye");
const fold = @import("fold.rye");

pub const stereo_fold_version = "20260815.033358";

/// Stereo fold's faults are exactly ALES80's own `FoldError` -- `BadGain` (zero denominator, below-unity gain, oversized numerator), `BadCeiling` (a ceiling outside [1, sample_max]), and `BadRange` (a span
/// outside the samples) -- reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new fault: it only runs the same wavefold on each channel after one shared validation.
pub const StereoFoldError = fold.FoldError;

/// Wavefold `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc` by the rational gain `num`/`den` (at least unity) into the magnitude `ceil`, the SAME triangle on each, running ALES80's proven mono `wavefold`. A
/// boosted sample below the ceiling passes clean, the excess above reflects back down the triangle. Validates both channels once against the shared parameters and length before either is mutated: refuses
/// `BadGain` / `BadCeiling` / `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_fold(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, num: u32, den: u32, ceil: i32) StereoFoldError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the gain, ceiling, and span are legal -- refused by name before any write, so a refusal never folds one channel and leaves the other dry. The gain and ceiling checks depend only on the
    // shared parameters; the span check on the shared length. These mirror ALES80's own guards exactly, so each mono wavefold below is pre-validated to succeed.
    if (den == 0) return error.BadGain;
    if (num < den) return error.BadGain;
    if (num > drive.max_drive_num) return error.BadGain;
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono wavefold is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME triangle on each.
    try fold.wavefold(&sc.left, start, count, num, den, ceil);
    try fold.wavefold(&sc.right, start, count, num, den, ceil);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- a fold writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO FOLD LAW -- each channel equals ALES80's mono wavefold with the same params byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting
//     length; THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW -- the ceiling and sign hold per channel, a 1:2 master's ratio COLLAPSES by reflection under a shared fold, an identical-channel master staying identical, the fold
//     reflecting where the hard drive pins; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- each refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, a unity fold below the ceiling the identity, count = 0 the identity ----

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO FOLD LAW -- each channel equals ALES80's mono wavefold with the same params, proven side by side over one span with different per-channel samples; a ×2 fold into a low ceiling passes the
    //    small samples and reflects the boosted large ones back down the triangle, per channel.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 2500, -3000 });
        try stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 4, 2, 1, 1500); // ×2 into 1500

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000 });
        try fold.wavefold(&ml, 0, 4, 2, 1, 1500);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 2500, -3000 });
        try fold.wavefold(&mr, 0, 4, 2, 1, 1500);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a fold writes values only. Proven at an even and an odd length.
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400 }, &[_]i16{ 400, 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_fold(&even, 0, 4, 2, 1, 500);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_fold(&odd, 0, 3, 3, 2, 250);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (odd.left.len != 3) return error.OddLength;
    }

    // 3a. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW -- the ceiling and sign discipline hold per channel: every output magnitude is at most the ceiling and every sign matches the input, on BOTH channels, under a hard
    //     boost that folds many times. This is the fold's honest promise (contrast the linear amplitude class, which preserved a ratio).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 800, -4000, 20 }, &[_]i16{ -30000, 7, -900 });
        const ceil: i16 = 1200;
        try stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 3, 5, 2, ceil); // ×2.5 into 1200
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        const li = [_]i16{ 800, -4000, 20 };
        const ri = [_]i16{ -30000, 7, -900 };
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 3) : (i += 1) {
            if (l[i] > ceil or l[i] < -ceil) return error.LeftCeilingCrossed;
            if (r[i] > ceil or r[i] < -ceil) return error.RightCeilingCrossed;
            if (!((li[i] >= 0 and l[i] >= 0) or (li[i] <= 0 and l[i] <= 0))) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if (!((ri[i] >= 0 and r[i] >= 0) or (ri[i] <= 0 and r[i] <= 0))) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 3b. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW, the ratio COLLAPSES BY REFLECTION -- a 1:2 master (right twice left) folded through a ceiling does NOT keep its 1:2 ratio, because a fold is nonlinear and reflecting:
    //     1000 sits below the 1500 ceiling and passes clean, while 2000's excess reflects to 3000 − 2000 = 1000, so both channels land on 1000 -- the ratio collapsed to 1:1, and NEITHER is at the ceiling
    //     (the honest contrast with ALES132's hard drive, which would have PINNED both to the ceiling).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, 1000, 1000 }, &[_]i16{ 2000, 2000, 2000 });
        try stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 3, 1, 1, 1500); // unity into 1500: left 1000 clean, right 2000 reflects to 1000
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        if (l[0] != 1000 or r[0] != 1000) return error.ReflectPinWrong;
        if (@as(i32, r[0]) == 2 * @as(i32, l[0])) return error.RatioUnexpectedlyHeld; // the linear law does NOT hold here — that is the point
        if (l[0] == 1500 or r[0] == 1500) return error.FoldPinnedNotReflected; // neither at the ceiling — reflection, not the hard drive's pin
    }

    // 3c. an identical-channel master stays identical through the fold -- a shared triangle on equal inputs gives equal outputs (a mono-in-stereo signal never splits, even under a nonlinear map).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 });
        try stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 4, 4, 1, 1000);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3d. THE FOLD REFLECTS WHERE THE HARD DRIVE PINS -- below the ceiling the two agree, above it they diverge: a value boosted just past the ceiling folds BACK DOWN (strictly below the ceiling) while
    //     ALES132's hard drive pins it AT the ceiling. Proven on both channels against ALES78's own hard drive run per channel.
    {
        const sl = [_]i16{ 900, 1600 }; // 900 below the 1500 ceiling (agree), 1600 just past it (diverge)
        const sr = [_]i16{ -1600, -900 };
        var folded = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&folded, &sl, &sr);
        var pinned = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&pinned, &sl, &sr);
        try stereo_fold(&folded, 0, 2, 1, 1, 1500);
        try drive.drive(&pinned.left, 0, 2, 1, 1, 1500);
        try drive.drive(&pinned.right, 0, 2, 1, 1, 1500);
        // below the ceiling they agree (900 passes both ways).
        if (folded.left.samples()[0] != pinned.left.samples()[0]) return error.BelowCeilDisagree;
        // above the ceiling they diverge: fold reflects 1600 to 3000 − 1600 = 1400 (below the ceiling), drive pins it to 1500.
        if (folded.left.samples()[1] != 1400) return error.FoldReflectWrong;
        if (pinned.left.samples()[1] != 1500) return error.DrivePinWrong;
        if (folded.left.samples()[1] >= 1500) return error.FoldNotBelowCeil; // reflection lands strictly below the ceiling
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad gain -- a below-unity gain refuses BadGain, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        if (stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 3, 1, 2, 1000)) |_| return error.BadGainSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongBadGain;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 })) return error.BadGainLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.BadGainRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadGainUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad ceiling -- a zero ceiling refuses BadCeiling, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 3, 2, 1 });
        if (stereo_fold(&sc, 0, 3, 2, 1, 0)) |_| return error.BadCeilingSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongBadCeiling;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.BadCeilingLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 3, 2, 1 })) return error.BadCeilingRightMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span -- a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 });
        if (stereo_fold(&sc, 1, 3, 2, 1, 1000)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
    }

    // 4d. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- a unity fold whose samples all sit below the ceiling is the identity on both channels (nothing crosses the ceiling, nothing reflects), and count = 0 is the identity on both.
    {
        var below = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&below, &[_]i16{ 500, -900, 1400 }, &[_]i16{ -1400, 250, -500 });
        try stereo_fold(&below, 0, 3, 1, 1, 1500); // unity, every |sample| < 1500 — identity
        if (!eq(below.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 500, -900, 1400 })) return error.UnityLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(below.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -1400, 250, -500 })) return error.UnityRightChanged;

        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_fold(&noop, 1, 0, 4, 1, 1000); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-fold: the WAVEFOLDER CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED TRIANGLE, the reflecting corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class — wavefold a span in both channels of a master by the same " ++
        "gain into the same ceiling, running ALES80's proven mono wavefold on each, so one fold lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that " ++
        "left and right hold the same length. The nonlinear class holds as ALES132 stated it, honestly: a fold is not linear, so a shared triangle does NOT hold the inter-channel ratio — a 1:2 master " ++
        "folded through a ceiling can land both channels on one value (a 1000/2000 field folded through 1500 gives 1000/1000), the ratio collapsing not by pinning but by REFLECTION; that is what a stereo " ++
        "fold IS. What the shared map keeps, per channel, is the ceiling and sign discipline, an identical-channel master stays identical, and where the hard drive pins the excess the fold reflects it " ++
        "(the two agree below the ceiling and diverge above). It validates both channels once against the shared parameters and length before either is mutated (BadGain, BadCeiling, BadRange), so a " ++
        "refusal never folds one channel and leaves the other dry; FoldError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO FOLD LAW: each channel equals ALES80's mono wavefold with the same params byte " ++
        "for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW: the ceiling and sign hold per channel, a 1:2 master's ratio " ++
        "collapses by reflection under a shared fold, an identical-channel master staying identical, the fold reflecting where the hard drive pins. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both " ++
        "channels untouched and balanced, a unity fold below the ceiling the identity, count = 0 the identity. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips folded through the proven mono wavefold, the gain a " ++
        "rational num/den and the ceiling a sample magnitude not decibels; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
